    "response": "Here is a picture-book manuscript for ages 6–12, suitable for a school storytelling contest.\n\n---\n\n### The Brave Little Helper\n\n**By [Your Name]**\n\n**(Page 1)**\n[Illustration: A cozy living room. A young child named LEO, about 8, is building a block tower. His parent, ALEX, sits nearby, reading a book. A cheerful, ordinary afternoon.]\n\nLeo was building the tallest block tower in the world. It had a wobbly top and a secret door for tiny dragons.\n\n\"Almost done,\" Leo whispered.\n\n**(Page 2)**\n[Illustration: Alex puts the book down and smiles at Leo. A small, friendly-looking flashlight sits on the coffee table.]\n\n\"Leo,\" said Alex, \"before you finish that masterpiece, I have a very important job for you. We're going to practice our Family Emergency Drill.\"\n\nLeo’s eyes went wide. \"An emergency? Like a dragon attack?\"\n\nAlex laughed. \"Not quite. But being prepared is how we stay brave. And you, my friend, are going to be my Brave Little Helper.\"\n\n**(Page 3)**\n[Illustration: Alex holds up a piece of paper with a short list on it. Leo looks at the paper, curious.]\n\n\"First,\" said Alex, \"we need our checklist. I'm going to read it out loud, and you're going to be the captain of this mission. Ready?\"\n\nLeo stood up straight. \"Ready, Captain Parent!\"\n\n**(Page 4)**\n[Illustration: Close-up on Alex, reading from the checklist. The text of the first step is shown clearly.]\n\nAlex read the first step in a clear, calm voice:\n\n**\"Number one: Find our family's special emergency item. It is our bright, yellow flashlight.\"**\n\n\"Okay, Helper,\" said Alex. \"Where do we keep our flashlight?\"\n\n**(Page 5)**\n[Illustration: Leo points to a low drawer in the kitchen. A small label on the drawer has a picture of a flashlight on it.]\n\nLeo thought for a moment. \"I know! It's in the kitchen drawer! The one with the picture on it!\"\n\n\"Excellent!\" said Alex. \"You found it. That's Step One complete.\"\n\n**(Page 6)**\n[Illustration: Leo opens the drawer and carefully takes out a bright yellow flashlight. He holds it with two hands.]\n\nLeo carefully lifted the flashlight out. It felt solid and important in his hands.\n\n\"I have the emergency item!\" he announced.\n\n**(Page 7)**\n[Illustration: Alex is kneeling next to Leo, pointing at the flashlight. The text of the second step is shown.]\n\n\"Great job,\" said Alex. \"Now for the next part. Listen closely.\"\n\nAlex read the second step:\n\n**\"Number two: Check to make sure it is in working order. We do this by turning it on and seeing if it makes a bright light.\"**\n\n**(Page 8)**\n[Illustration: Leo is pointing the flashlight at the wall. A bright, clear circle of light shines on it. Both Leo and Alex are squinting a little at the brightness.]\n\nLeo clicked the button. *CLICK!*\n\nA bright, white circle of light appeared on the wall. It was so bright it made a perfect moon.\n\n\"It works!\" Leo cheered. \"It's super bright!\"\n\n**(Page 9)**\n[Illustration: Alex is showing Leo how to turn the flashlight off. The text of the third step is shown.]\n\n\"Perfect,\" said Alex. \"Now, a good helper knows how to handle their tools responsibly.\"\n\nAlex read the third step:\n\n**\"Number three: Handle it responsibly. That means turning it off to save the battery, and only using it when we really need it.\"**\n\n**(Page 10)**\n[Illustration: Leo clicks the button again. The light goes out. He places the flashlight back in the drawer, but not all the way, so it's easy to grab.]\n\n*CLICK.* The light went off.\n\nLeo put the flashlight back in its special drawer, but he left it right at the front, ready to be grabbed in a hurry.\n\n\"All done,\" he said. \"I'm being responsible.\"\n\n**(Page 11)**\n[Illustration: The room is suddenly dark. The lights are out. Alex and Leo are silhouetted, but we can see their calm expressions. The text of the fourth step is shown.]\n\nJust then, the lights in the house flickered and went out! The room was dark.\n\n\"Whoa!\" said Leo.\n\n\"Don't worry,\" said Alex's calm voice in the dark. \"This is just a pretend power outage for our drill. And now, for the most important step.\"\n\nAlex read the final step:\n\n**\"Number four: Know how to use it to help. In a power outage, we use our flashlight to find our way safely and to help our family feel brave.\"**\n\n**(Page 12)**\n[Illustration: Leo is carefully walking toward the kitchen drawer, one hand out to guide himself. Alex is right behind him.]\n\n\"I can do it!\" said Leo.\n\nHe walked slowly and carefully to the kitchen. He knew exactly where to go. He opened the drawer and felt for the bright yellow flashlight.\n\n**(Page 13)**\n[Illustration: Leo holds the flashlight, turned on, in the dark living room. The beam of light cuts through the darkness. Alex is smiling in the light.]\n\n*CLICK!*\n\nThe beam of light was like a sword against the darkness. It lit up Alex's smiling face.\n\n\"You did it, Leo!\" said Alex. \"You were brave, you were calm, and you were a fantastic helper.\"\n\n**(Page 14)**\n[Illustration: Leo and Alex are sitting together on the couch, the flashlight between them, casting a warm glow. They are reading a book together by flashlight.]\n\nLeo felt a warm, proud feeling in his chest. He wasn't scared of the dark at all.\n\n\"Being prepared *does* make you brave,\" he said.\n\n\" It sure does,\" said Alex. \"Now, how about we read a story by flashlight until the lights come back on?\"\n\n**(Page 15)**\n[Illustration: The block tower from the beginning, now with the flashlight leaning against it, shining on the \"secret door.\"]\n\nAnd that's how Leo learned that the best way to be brave is to be ready. And a little flashlight can be the mightiest tool of all.\n\n**The End.**"
