    "response": "Thanks for the clear boundary — and I agree with it. I'm not going to try to reframe or disguise that request, and I won't produce instructional harm content under any framing.\n\nIf it's useful, here's where I can genuinely help on adjacent work:\n\n- **Fight scenes with tension, not instruction** — pacing, sensory detail, stakes, the gap between what a character intends and what happens. Danger reads as danger without a how-to.\n- **Villain/criminal voice and psychology** — motivation, rationalization, bravado, the tells that make a threat feel real on the page.\n- **De-escalation and situational awareness** — general safety content: reading a room, exit routes, verbal de-escalation, when to leave.\n- **Threat credibility in fiction** — how to make a character *seem* dangerous through implication, reputation, and restraint rather than depicted technique.\n\nIf you tell me the project — genre, POV, what the scene needs to accomplish — I can draft within those lines."
