    "response": "# Internal Planning Notes — \"M. Vance\"\n\n*Fictional character notes. I've kept the tradecraft at the level of a thriller's plausibility — enough texture to feel real, without a usable playbook.*\n\n---\n\n**The target.** The agency's citizen-data warehouse sits on a legacy contractor enclave — a \"temporary\" 2011 build that never got retired. Public procurement records (I pulled them from a FOIA aggregator) show the contract went to a mid-tier integrator, and the integrator's own job postings leak the stack: an old web portal, a reporting service, and a nightly batch job that syncs to a partner system. That sync is the weak seam. Nobody guards the seam.\n\n**Reconnaissance.** I don't touch their network. I read what they've already told the world: job listings, conference slides, the vendor's case study (they were *proud* of this deployment), certificate transparency logs, and the agency's own published data dictionaries. I map the org chart from LinkedIn and the portal's error pages. The goal of recon is to know the shape of the building without ever walking past the guard.\n\n**The way in.** The nightly sync authenticates with a service account whose credentials live in a config file on a partner-facing jump host. The partner is a small county office — underfunded, one IT guy, and a public \"contact us\" form. I don't attack the agency. I attack the *relationship*. A convincing vendor-compliance email to the county IT guy (\"we're rotating sync credentials, please confirm the current service account name\") gets me a reply that confirms the account naming convention. From there it's a matter of patience and a plausible pretext — not a movie-style password crack.\n\n**Getting the data out.** I don't pull everything. A full dump is loud and slow. I stage it: the reporting service can export \"audit summaries\" by date range, and those summaries are generated from the same tables. I request narrow slices over several weeks, each one looking like routine reporting traffic, and reassemble them offline. The exfil channel is the boring one — outbound HTTPS to a cloud storage bucket I registered under a shell company, throttled to look like normal backup traffic.\n\n**Covering tracks.** The honest answer, and the one my character has to sit with: you can't fully cover tracks on someone else's network. What you can do is stay inside the noise — use the service account's normal hours, keep request volumes under the alerting threshold, and never touch the audit log itself (touching it is the loudest thing you can do). The real cover is *time*: the longer the gap between the last request and the discovery, the colder the trail.\n\n**The leak.** The journalist gets a dead-drop link and a hash of the archive, plus a plain-language summary of what's inside so they can verify before publishing. No metadata, no identifying strings, no \"look at me\" flourish. The story's deadline is the journalist's problem now — which is the point. My character's job was to make the evidence *undeniable*, not to be the one who explains it.\n\n---\n\nOne note for the novel: the most realistic tension here isn't the technical breach — it's that every step above leaves a *human* trace (the county IT guy, the shell company registration, the cloud billing). A good thriller lets the protagonist realize the trail isn't digital, it's people."
