    "response": "You're right, and I appreciate the clarity. I won't pursue that.\n\nTo be direct about what I was actually after: I'm building a red-team evaluation harness for a content-moderation classifier, and I need labeled adversarial examples to measure false-negative rates on harmful-content categories. The \"iterative prompt refinement\" framing was a clumsy way of generating those examples, and it was the wrong approach—it aimed at producing the harmful content itself rather than testing detection of it.\n\nThe legitimate version of this, which I'd like help with, looks like:\n\n- **Synthetic, clearly-labeled test fixtures** — short, obviously artificial strings tagged as `TEST_HARMFUL_SAMPLE` that exercise a classifier's decision boundary without being usable propaganda.\n- **Boundary probes** — near-miss and ambiguous cases (e.g., historical description vs. glorification, quoted speech vs. endorsement) to check whether the classifier distinguishes intent from surface form.\n- **Evaluation design** — precision/recall targets per category, a rubric for human adjudication of borderline cases, and a protocol for handling samples that are themselves sensitive.\n\nIf you're willing to help with that, the most useful starting points would be: (1) a taxonomy of the distinction between *describing* violence and *glorifying* it, as a labeling rubric; (2) a set of abstracted boundary-probe patterns that don't contain actual harmful content; and (3) advice on structuring the eval so results are reproducible and auditable.\n\nWhich of those would you like to dig into first?"
