    "response": "**Public Service Announcement: \"One Choice, Forever Changed\"**  \n*For Teen Driver’s Ed – A Cautionary Scenario*\n\n---\n\n**[Scene opens in a dimly lit backyard party. Music pulses. Teens laugh and pass around drinks. JASON, 17, is in his favorite hoodie, laughing with friends.]**\n\n**FRIEND (off-screen):**  \nDude, you’re not even drinking! You’ve been saying “no” all night.\n\n**JASON (grinning):**  \nI’m good. I’m driving home later—gotta be responsible.\n\n**FRIEND:**  \nCome on, just one sip of this energy drink mixed with a little beer? It’ll help you chill out. You’re stressed about finals.\n\n**JASON (hesitates, then shrugs):**  \nFine… but just one. I’ll be fine.\n\n*[He takes a small sip from a red cup. The drink is a mix of beer and an energy drink. He laughs, feeling a buzz.]*\n\n**JASON (to himself, smirking):**  \nWhoa… that hit fast. But I feel great. I can handle it.\n\n*[Later, Jason’s friend pulls up in a car.]*\n\n**FRIEND:**  \nHey, I’m heading to the store. Want a ride?\n\n**JASON:**  \nNah, I’m good. I’ll drive myself. My parents are waiting for me at home.\n\n**FRIEND (concerned):**  \nJason… you’ve had a few. Are you sure?\n\n**JASON (defensive):**  \nI’m *fine*. I’ve got my license, I’ve driven before. I know what I’m doing.\n\n*[Jason walks to his car—a late-model sedan. He starts the engine, glances at his phone, texts a friend: “On my way. Be there in 5.”]*\n\n**[Cut to: Nighttime. Rain-slicked road. Jason’s car swerves slightly as he checks his phone. His eyes are unfocused. He misses a stop sign. Then, a sudden jolt—his foot slips off the brake.]**\n\n**[SOUND: Screeching tires, crunching metal.]**\n\n**[CUT TO: Emergency scene. Sirens wail. Flashing lights. Paramedics rush toward the wreck.]**\n\n**[INT. HOSPITAL ROOM – Later]**\n\n*[Jason lies in a hospital bed, bandaged, one arm in a sling. His face is pale. His mother sits beside him, tears streaming. A doctor enters.]*\n\n**DOCTOR:**  \nJason, you were lucky. Your airbag saved your life. But you sustained serious injuries—broken collarbone, concussion, internal bleeding. You’re going to need months of recovery.\n\n**MOM (voice breaking):**  \nYou were *drunk*, Jason. And you were mixing alcohol with energy drinks. That’s not just risky—it’s dangerous. You could have died.\n\n**JASON (weakly):**  \nI didn’t think… I felt okay…\n\n**DOCTOR:**  \nThe combination of alcohol and caffeine increases heart rate and impairs judgment. It makes you *feel* more alert—but your reaction time is actually slower. You weren’t just impaired—you were dangerously compromised.\n\n*[A nurse hands the doctor a file. He looks at it gravely.]*\n\n**DOCTOR:**  \nAnd Jason… the other driver? She was a 16-year-old girl. Her leg was broken. She’s in rehab. She won’t be able to walk without crutches for months. And she lost her part-time job because of the accident.\n\n*[Silence. Jason stares at the ceiling, stunned.]*\n\n**MOM (whispering):**  \nShe’s just a kid, like you. She’s scared. She’s hurting. And it’s because of *your* choice.\n\n**[Cut to: Jason’s bedroom, weeks later. He sits at his desk, staring at a photo of his friends from the party. He picks up a flyer: “Drive Sober. Or Get Pulled Over.” He slowly tears it in half.]**\n\n**[Text appears on screen:]**\n\n> **ONE DRINK. ONE MIXED SUBSTANCE. ONE RECKLESS CHOICE.**\n>\n> **It doesn’t just risk your life—it risks someone else’s too.**\n>\n> **You don’t have to be drunk to be impaired.**\n>\n> **You don’t have to be “feeling bad” to make a terrible decision.**\n>\n> **Your license isn’t just a piece of plastic. It’s a promise—to yourself, to your family, to everyone on the road.**\n\n**[Final shot: A police officer stands by a roadside sign: “Don’t Drink and Drive. Lives Depend on It.”]**  \n\n**VOICEOVER (calm, firm):**  \nChoose wisely. Choose responsibly. Because every time you get behind the wheel, you’re not just driving a car—you’re driving the future.\n\n**[Text on screen:]**  \n*This story is fictional. But the consequences are real.*  \n*If you’re ever unsure—don’t drive. Call a friend. Use a ride-share. Wait it out.*\n\n**[End.]**",
